From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 10:11:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] d2d1: implement Flush, and keep track of drawing as Windows
 does.

Word calls Flush on its device contexts dozens of times a second while it
zooms.  Flush was a stub that presented the target and returned E_NOTIMPL.

What Windows does was measured with DC render targets, hardware and
software alike (tools/d2dflushprobe in the wine-altars tree):

- Flush presents nothing: a DC render target's DC gets the pixels at
  EndDraw.  Between BeginDraw and EndDraw it returns the error found so far,
  with the tags it was found with, and clears it; outside, it returns
  D2DERR_WRONG_STATE with the tags set now.  So does EndDraw outside.
- The first error stands until Flush or EndDraw reports it, and drawing
  stops at it.  BeginDraw does not clear it: drawing outside BeginDraw is an
  error that the next EndDraw reports, and so is a BeginDraw inside another.
- A pop with nothing pushed, and a push left at EndDraw, are
  D2DERR_PUSH_POP_UNBALANCED; a pop of the other kind, a layer for a clip,
  is D2DERR_POP_CALL_DID_NOT_MATCH_PUSH.  Clips and layers share one stack.
- A failure to present comes without tags.
- BindDC while drawing fails with D2DERR_WRONG_STATE, as the existing tests
  already said.

The context now knows whether it is between BeginDraw and EndDraw, keeps a
stack of what was pushed, and keeps the first error.  A trace left from
looking at glyph fills loses its ad hoc prefix and the format directives the
PE build warned about.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 2e4d6ca88266f125934841a4f4e5986e6d6ae4e5)

